1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,900
A ceiling fan blade.

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:03,808
The second hand of a clock.

3
00:00:03,808 --> 00:00:05,668
A giant wheel at the fair.

4
00:00:06,068 --> 00:00:09,186
Each one goes round and
round at a steady speed.

5
00:00:09,186 --> 00:00:11,200
So is anything really changing?

6
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:14,026
Diya switches on the kitchen mixer.

7
00:00:14,026 --> 00:00:16,623
A drop of batter rides round the jar.

8
00:00:16,973 --> 00:00:18,700
Its speed never changes.

9
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:20,703
So is the drop speeding up?

10
00:00:20,703 --> 00:00:22,594
Is it accelerating at all?

11
00:00:23,094 --> 00:00:26,625
By the end of this video, you
will see why the answer is yes.

12
00:00:27,275 --> 00:00:29,687
So what is uniform circular motion?

13
00:00:30,037 --> 00:00:33,672
An object moves along a
circle, at a constant speed.

14
00:00:33,672 --> 00:00:36,355
Uniform means the speed stays the same.

15
00:00:36,755 --> 00:00:38,828
Every turn takes the same time.

16
00:00:38,828 --> 00:00:41,937
This time is called the
time period, capital T.

17
00:00:42,537 --> 00:00:44,937
Let's run one in our motion notebook.

18
00:00:44,937 --> 00:00:48,990
A ball goes round a two metre
circle, once every four seconds.

19
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:51,297
Watch the speed in the readout.

20
00:00:51,297 --> 00:00:55,545
It stays at three point one four
metres per second, all the way round.

21
00:00:55,945 --> 00:00:58,202
Now watch the blue velocity arrow.

22
00:00:58,202 --> 00:01:02,120
It always touches the circle,
at right angles to the radius.

23
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:04,198
Its length stays the same.

24
00:01:04,198 --> 00:01:06,148
But its direction keeps turning.

25
00:01:06,548 --> 00:01:09,321
The graph shows the two
parts of the velocity.

26
00:01:09,321 --> 00:01:12,229
They rise and fall, while
the speed stays flat.

27
00:01:12,729 --> 00:01:15,227
Let's slow it down and
compare two moments.

28
00:01:15,577 --> 00:01:18,990
Here is the velocity now, and
here it is a little later.

29
00:01:18,990 --> 00:01:20,533
Slide them tail to tail.

30
00:01:20,933 --> 00:01:24,211
The arrow that joins them
is the change in velocity.

31
00:01:24,211 --> 00:01:27,394
It is not zero, and it
points toward the centre.

32
00:01:28,044 --> 00:01:30,356
Now, how fast does the ball go?

33
00:01:30,356 --> 00:01:31,851
One step at a time.

34
00:01:32,201 --> 00:01:33,101
Step one.

35
00:01:33,013 --> 00:01:36,172
In one turn, the ball
covers the whole circle.

36
00:01:36,172 --> 00:01:39,222
That distance is the
circumference, two pi r.

37
00:01:39,972 --> 00:01:40,904
Step two.

38
00:01:40,904 --> 00:01:43,712
One turn takes one time period, T.

39
00:01:43,712 --> 00:01:47,246
So the speed v equals
two pi r divided by T.

40
00:01:47,996 --> 00:01:48,955
Step three.

41
00:01:48,955 --> 00:01:52,810
Frequency, nu, is the number
of turns in each second.

42
00:01:52,810 --> 00:01:54,696
So nu equals one over T.

43
00:01:55,446 --> 00:01:56,346
Step four.

44
00:01:56,337 --> 00:01:57,699
Put them together.

45
00:01:57,699 --> 00:01:59,398
v equals two pi r nu.

46
00:01:59,398 --> 00:02:02,972
Metres times turns per second
gives metres per second.

47
00:02:03,722 --> 00:02:05,531
For our ball, that is pi.

48
00:02:05,531 --> 00:02:08,281
About three point one
four metres per second.

49
00:02:08,981 --> 00:02:10,029
Remember this.

50
00:02:10,029 --> 00:02:14,405
In uniform circular motion, the speed
is constant, but the velocity is

51
00:02:14,405 --> 00:02:15,305
not.

52
00:02:15,604 --> 00:02:19,143
Let's compare two kinds of
steady motion, side by side.

53
00:02:19,493 --> 00:02:22,734
A ball rolls straight at
three metres per second.

54
00:02:22,734 --> 00:02:24,425
Its arrow never changes.

55
00:02:24,425 --> 00:02:25,596
No acceleration.

56
00:02:25,996 --> 00:02:29,008
Another ball circles at
three metres per second.

57
00:02:29,008 --> 00:02:30,635
Its arrow keeps turning.

58
00:02:30,635 --> 00:02:32,511
So there is an acceleration.

59
00:02:33,111 --> 00:02:34,218
Now you try.

60
00:02:34,218 --> 00:02:38,376
A car takes a curve at a steady
sixty kilometres per hour.

61
00:02:38,376 --> 00:02:39,859
Is it accelerating?

62
00:02:39,859 --> 00:02:41,234
Pause and decide.

63
00:02:44,434 --> 00:02:45,334
Yes!

64
00:02:44,956 --> 00:02:48,472
Its speed is steady, but
its direction is changing.

65
00:02:48,472 --> 00:02:51,201
A change in velocity is an acceleration.

66
00:02:51,851 --> 00:02:54,440
But which way does this
acceleration point?

67
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:55,615
Follow the links.

68
00:02:55,965 --> 00:02:56,865
One.

69
00:02:56,475 --> 00:02:59,392
The velocity always
points along the tangent.

70
00:02:59,792 --> 00:03:00,692
Two.

71
00:03:00,254 --> 00:03:04,723
As the ball moves on, the arrow turns
a little, always toward the centre.

72
00:03:05,123 --> 00:03:06,023
Three.

73
00:03:05,752 --> 00:03:09,197
So the change in velocity
points toward the centre.

74
00:03:09,597 --> 00:03:10,497
Four.

75
00:03:10,134 --> 00:03:13,135
Acceleration points the
same way as that change.

76
00:03:13,135 --> 00:03:15,014
So it points to the centre.

77
00:03:15,014 --> 00:03:18,005
We call it centripetal,
meaning centre seeking.

78
00:03:18,605 --> 00:03:19,505
One more.

79
00:03:19,386 --> 00:03:22,574
A ball whirls on a string,
and the string snaps.

80
00:03:22,574 --> 00:03:24,487
Which way does the ball fly?

81
00:03:24,487 --> 00:03:25,657
Pause and think.

82
00:03:28,857 --> 00:03:30,076
Along the tangent!

83
00:03:30,076 --> 00:03:34,125
At that moment its velocity points
along the tangent, and nothing pulls

84
00:03:34,125 --> 00:03:36,059
it toward the centre any more.

85
00:03:36,709 --> 00:03:38,497
Now, one worked example.

86
00:03:38,497 --> 00:03:39,986
Back to Diya's mixer.

87
00:03:40,336 --> 00:03:43,106
A drop sits six centimetres
from the centre.

88
00:03:43,106 --> 00:03:45,783
The jar makes twenty turns in two seconds.

89
00:03:45,783 --> 00:03:47,636
Find nu, T and the speed.

90
00:03:48,136 --> 00:03:51,736
Twenty turns in two seconds
is ten turns each second.

91
00:03:51,736 --> 00:03:53,279
So nu is ten hertz.

92
00:03:54,029 --> 00:03:55,503
T is one over nu.

93
00:03:55,503 --> 00:03:57,724
That is zero point one second.

94
00:03:58,474 --> 00:04:00,711
v equals two pi r over T.

95
00:04:00,711 --> 00:04:05,639
That is two pi times zero point
zero six, over zero point one.

96
00:04:06,389 --> 00:04:09,797
That is about three point
seven seven metres per second.

97
00:04:09,797 --> 00:04:13,680
A steady speed, but the direction
turns ten times every second.

98
00:04:14,330 --> 00:04:15,793
Now, the exam trap.

99
00:04:16,143 --> 00:04:20,567
Many students write: the speed is
constant, so the acceleration is zero.

100
00:04:20,917 --> 00:04:23,982
But in a circle, the
direction keeps changing.

101
00:04:23,982 --> 00:04:27,753
So the velocity changes, and
the acceleration is not zero.

102
00:04:27,753 --> 00:04:29,466
It points to the centre.

103
00:04:30,116 --> 00:04:31,534
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:31,534 --> 00:04:35,922
As short concept questions: what stays
constant, what changes, and speed

105
00:04:35,922 --> 00:04:37,092
from the period.

106
00:04:37,692 --> 00:04:38,885
Exam level now.

107
00:04:38,885 --> 00:04:43,688
A particle moves on a circle at a
steady five metres per second, and turns

108
00:04:43,688 --> 00:04:46,277
through one hundred and twenty degrees.

109
00:04:46,277 --> 00:04:50,706
Find the change in speed, and the
size of the change in velocity.

110
00:04:50,706 --> 00:04:51,949
Pause and solve.

111
00:04:57,349 --> 00:04:58,615
The answer is C.

112
00:04:59,115 --> 00:05:02,688
The speed is still five, so
the change in speed is zero.

113
00:05:03,188 --> 00:05:05,967
Put the two velocity arrows tail to tail.

114
00:05:05,967 --> 00:05:09,665
The angle between them is one
hundred and twenty degrees.

115
00:05:10,165 --> 00:05:14,594
So the change is the root of twenty
five, plus twenty five, minus fifty

116
00:05:14,594 --> 00:05:15,720
cos one twenty.

117
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:20,035
That is root seventy five, about
eight point seven metres per second.

118
00:05:20,635 --> 00:05:22,094
Here is your recap.

119
00:05:22,094 --> 00:05:23,593
Screenshot this card.

120
00:05:26,793 --> 00:05:29,630
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

121
00:05:30,030 --> 00:05:30,930
One.

122
00:05:30,585 --> 00:05:35,465
Why is a body in uniform circular
motion accelerating, even at a steady

123
00:05:35,465 --> 00:05:36,365
speed?

124
00:05:37,030 --> 00:05:37,930
Two.

125
00:05:37,527 --> 00:05:40,141
A string snaps while a ball goes round.

126
00:05:40,141 --> 00:05:42,102
Which way does the ball fly?

127
00:05:43,002 --> 00:05:43,902
Three.

128
00:05:43,559 --> 00:05:47,334
How do you find the speed from
the radius and the time period?

129
00:05:48,434 --> 00:05:52,560
Next time: the second hand and
the hour hand share one clock.

130
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:53,943
Which turns faster?

131
00:05:53,943 --> 00:05:57,118
We meet angular displacement
and angular velocity.
